From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anton Palgunov <toxblh@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 20:57:27 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 64/99] v8: e2k: Smi fast paths of arithmetic and compares in
 baseline code

Baseline code called a *_Generic_Baseline builtin for every arithmetic
bytecode: a call through a table and a register, the Smi path, the update
of the feedback embedded in the bytecode (two table loads one after the
other) and a return through a register, 50-58 cycles for an operation of
one or two. In a loop of a function called once, which is what a page in
efficiency mode runs, that was two thirds of the time.

Sparkplug+ is now built for e2k (it was x64, arm64 and loong64), and where
the embedded feedback covers Smi operands with a Smi result the baseline
compiler emits the operation on the tagged values itself: + - * | & ^ << >>
>>> with a register or a constant, ++ and --, and the six compares. The
checks are rare branches (the tag of both operands in one test, overflow by
cmpodb of the result, a product by smulx and its sign extension, -0 from
the signs), the builtin stays as the slow path, the feedback is left as it
is. A compare followed by JumpIfTrue or JumpIfFalse jumps on the compare
and passes that bytecode by; the boolean is chosen by a merge, without a
branch. The upstream paths for ++, -- and compares (sparkplug_inline_smi,
off by default) are used in this e2k form too.

Unlike upstream the path is taken for any numeric feedback that includes
Smis, not only for kSignedSmall: the slow path is the generic builtin, and
a sum that overflows once in a thousand iterations has made the feedback
kNumber before the loop is compiled. --no-sparkplug-smi-fast-paths turns it
off. Patching of calls to typed builtins (--sparkplug-plus proper) needs
short builtin calls and stays off.

bench.js intLoop under --no-opt: 4608 -> about 1100 ms.
